Done. `bench.py` now runs in about 0.13s total instead of 8.2s (rolling median 3.96s → 0.10s; distinct counts 4.25s → 0.03s).

What changed in [analytics.py](/Users/eranshir/Documents/Projects/deepseek/bench/hard/work/deepseek_h1_perf_t1/analytics.py):

- `rolling_median` went from re-sorting every window (O(n·w log w)) to a two-heap sliding median with lazy deletion (O(n log w)). The window is kept split into lower/upper halves; entries are `(value, index)` pairs so duplicates stay orderable, and departing elements are flagged and popped only when they reach the heap top.
- `window_distinct_counts` went from rebuilding a `set` per window (O(n·w)) to a single sliding `Counter` (O(n)), adding the incoming value and removing the outgoing one each step.

Signatures are untouched, and I verified exact behavioral parity against the original implementation: randomized fuzz tests, edge cases (empty lists, `window > len`, `window == len`, sizes 1/2, duplicates, floats, negatives, `-0.0`), adversarial patterns (ascending, descending, all-equal, high-duplicate), large inputs, and error behavior — `ValueError("window must be positive")` for non-positive windows, and the same `TypeError` for non-integer windows.
